            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Writes the values of a source tensor INTO a copy of a target tensor, at positions you give.\n\nIt is the reverse of selecting: t.index_select reads values out at positions, t.scatter puts values in at positions. Everything the positions do not name keeps the target's value. Use it to place readings into a larger frame, to build a one-hot vector, or to undo a t.argsort.\n\nHOW THE POSITIONS ARE READ:\nFor a 1-D target, source value number i goes to position indices[i]. Only as many source values are used as there are indices, so the source may be longer than the index list but not shorter. For 2-D and more, indices must have as many dimensions as the target, and along dim each index says where in that row (dim 1) or column (dim 0) its source value goes - the same layout t.take_along_dim reads, written the other way. If two indices name the same position, only one of the values ends up there, and PyTorch does not promise which.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.scatter <dim: int>\nDefault dim 0. The dim box is always shown.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.scatter 0\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor to scatter into:\nThe target (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). It is not changed: the result is a new tensor. Sends on arrival - this is the only inlet that sends.\n\nindices in:\nWhere to write, as whole numbers (a list, an array or a tensor). A position past the end prints an error in the console and nothing is sent. Cold: stored for the next target.\n\nsource in:\nThe values to write, converted to a float32 tensor. Cold. Its dtype must match the target's: a float32 target is the normal case; an integer target (from t.arange with int64, say) is refused with an error.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich dimension the indices run along. Negative counts from the end (-1 is the last dimension). A dimension the target does not have sends nothing.\n\nNothing is sent until both indices and source have arrived.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe target with the source values written in.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe source is four values, 0.25 0.5 0.75 1 (top strip). A metro sends t.zeros 10 - ten zeros - as the target ten times a second. Click a list of positions: 0 1 2 3 puts the four values at the start; 9 6 3 0 spreads them backwards from the end; 2 2 5 5 writes two values to each of two places, and only one of each pair survives.\n\nRELATED:\nt.scatter_hold keeps its tensor between messages and takes index, value pairs - the easier node for building a tensor up piece by piece. t.index_select and t.take_along_dim read values out the same way. np.edit writes into a region of a numpy array.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
